statement:
Let \(k\ge1\), \(v\ge0\), and \(T\ge0\) be integers, let \(\beta\ge0\) be a finite real number, and let \(B_i\ge2\) and \(C_{4,i}\ge2B_i-1\) be integers for \(i\in[k]\). For these coordinates put \(g_i=(0,1,B_i,C_{4,i})\) on \(\lbrace0,1,2,3\rbrace\). For \(k<i\le k+v\), put \(g_i(0)=0\) and \(g_i(1)=1\). The arithmetic ambient dimension, including height, is \(N=k+v+1\). Define
$$
\begin{aligned}
D_{\rm full}&=\lbrace0,1,2,3\rbrace^k\times\lbrace0,1\rbrace^v,
&\Gamma(\mathbf x)&=\sum_{i=1}^{k+v}g_i(x_i),\\
f_E&=\begin{cases}\displaystyle\sum_{t=E}^T e^{-\beta t},&0\le E\le T,\\0,&E>T,\end{cases}
&Z_{\rm full}&=\sum_{\mathbf x\in D_{\rm full}}f_{\Gamma(\mathbf x)}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{1}
$$
Choose disjoint sets \(P,J\subseteq[k]\), where \(m=|P|\ge1\). Assume that \(B_i=b\ge2\) for every \(i\in P\), and \(B_i\ge b\) for every \(i\in J\). No comparison between \(b\) and \(B_i\) is imposed for \(i\notin P\cup J\). Set
$$
\begin{aligned}
D_{\rm sel}&=\lbrace\mathbf y\in D_{\rm full}:y_i\ne3\text{ for every }i\in J\rbrace,
&Z_{\rm sel}&=\sum_{\mathbf y\in D_{\rm sel}}f_{\Gamma(\mathbf y)},\\
A_P(\mathbf y)&=|\lbrace i\in P:y_i=1\rbrace|,
&Q_{P,J}(\mathbf y)&=|\lbrace i\in P\cup J:y_i\in\lbrace2,3\rbrace\rbrace|,\\
F_{P,J}(\mathbf y)&=\frac{Q_{P,J}(\mathbf y)\bigl(Q_{P,J}(\mathbf y)+A_P(\mathbf y)/b\bigr)}m,
&\mathcal B_{P,J}&=\frac1{Z_{\rm sel}}\sum_{\mathbf y\in D_{\rm sel}}
f_{\Gamma(\mathbf y)}e^{64F_{P,J}(\mathbf y)}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{2}
$$
The definitions of \(A_P,Q_{P,J}\) also apply to full source vectors. In a selective target, the occurrences of 3 counted by \(Q_{P,J}\) can lie only in \(P\). Then
$$
\frac{Z_{\rm full}}{Z_{\rm sel}}
\le\mathcal B_{P,J}
=\mathbb E_{\rm sel}\exp\!\left(
\frac{64Q_{P,J}(Q_{P,J}+A_P/b)}m\right),
\tag{3}
$$
where \(\mathbb E_{\rm sel}\) uses exactly the original selective spatial marginal \(f_{\Gamma(\mathbf y)}/Z_{\rm sel}\).

The scalar tails \(f_E\), original tilt \(\beta\), cap \(T\), and four-site costs have the meanings used in accepted `9f6ebd76c13f58a8` and `a2136c3725dae630`. The pool of the latter fact was the entire global minimum-cost pool; here \(P\) is a chosen eligible pool and \(b\) need not be the global minimum. Its source counts \(a,z\) map respectively to the present \(A_P(\mathbf x)\) and \( |\lbrace i\in P:x_i=0\rbrace|\). Its global two-count \(s\), and the global target two-count \(q\) in `9f6ebd76c13f58a8`, mean \( |\lbrace i\in[k]:x_i=2\rbrace|\) or its target analogue. They are not \(Q_{P,J}\), which is a new restricted count and also counts retained pool threes. Below a local scalar \(q\) always explicitly abbreviates \(Q_{P,J}\) of the indicated vector. Likewise, the present \(\ell\) counts threes only in \(J\); retained threes outside \(J\) are unchanged. The two normalizers in (1)-(2) are those of full and selective deletion, respectively, rather than the predecessor's full/all-ternary pair. No predecessor's cap-budget bound is asserted for these new objects. The locked mixed set \(S\), and locked \(n,d,C,\Lambda,W,c_0\), retain their meanings.

Here is the precise direct moment use of (3). For \(a\in\lbrace\mathrm{full},\mathrm{sel}\rbrace\), define the complete original law
$$
\mathcal A_a=\lbrace(\mathbf x,t):\mathbf x\in D_a, t\in\mathbb Z,
\Gamma(\mathbf x)\le t\le T\rbrace,
\qquad p^a_{(\mathbf x,t)}=e^{-\beta t}/Z_a.
\tag{4}
$$
For any finite law \(p\) in its stated ambient space, use the ambient closed-affine-halfspace definitions \(\tau_p(\mathbf z)=\inf_{H\ni\mathbf z}p(H)\), with nonzero halfspace normal, and \(M_3(p)=\sum_{\mathbf z}p_{\mathbf z}\tau_p(\mathbf z)^3\). Both laws in (4) use \(\mathbb R^N\). One has
$$
M_3(p^{\rm full})\ge\mathcal B_{P,J}^{-4}M_3(p^{\rm sel}).
\tag{5}
$$
Consequently, for every \(\kappa>0\) and finite \(L_0\ge1\), the two assumptions \(M_3(p^{\rm sel})\ge\kappa8^{-N}\) and \(\mathcal B_{P,J}\le L_0\) imply \(M_3(p^{\rm full})\ge\kappa L_0^{-4}8^{-N}\), with exactly the same ambient halfspaces and the normalizers in (4).

To compose this with accepted `8668ed0d436b9cba`, let \(s=|J|\), \(r=k+v-s\ge1\), and regard all coordinates outside \(J\), including every binary companion, as the core. Its spatial set is their full product box \(D_{\rm core}\subseteq\mathbb Z^r\), its energy \(h_{\rm core}\) is the sum of their unchanged costs, and its spatial weights \(q_{\mathbf x}\) in that predecessor are all 1. For \(0\le U\le T\), set
$$
\begin{aligned}
Z_{\rm core}(U)&=\sum_{\mathbf x\in D_{\rm core}}
\sum_{t'=h_{\rm core}(\mathbf x)}^U e^{-\beta t'},
&p_{\rm core}^{(U)}(\mathbf x,t')&=e^{-\beta t'}/Z_{\rm core}(U),\\
\Gamma_J(\mathbf u)&=\sum_{i\in J}g_i(u_i),
&\pi_{\mathbf u}&=\frac{e^{-\beta\Gamma_J(\mathbf u)}
Z_{\rm core}(T-\Gamma_J(\mathbf u))}{Z_{\rm sel}}
\quad(\Gamma_J(\mathbf u)\le T).
\end{aligned}
\tag{6}
$$
The first probability in (6) is defined on the complete core support \(h_{\rm core}(\mathbf x)\le t'\le U\); an inner sum with lower endpoint exceeding its upper endpoint is empty. The tail vector \(\mathbf u\) ranges over \(\lbrace0,1,2\rbrace^J\). Thus \(\pi\) is its marginal under \(p^{\rm sel}\), with the original cap and tilt. The exact map to `8668ed0d436b9cba` is its \(\Gamma=\Gamma_J\), \(Z=Z_{\rm sel}\), \(p=p^{\rm sel}\), with its other displayed core objects identical to (6). Its conclusion and (5) give
$$
M_3(p^{\rm full})\ge
\frac{c_{\rm ext}\,8^{-s}}{\mathcal B_{P,J}^{4}}
\left(\sum_{\substack{\mathbf u\in\lbrace0,1,2\rbrace^J\\\Gamma_J(\mathbf u)\le T}}
\pi_{\mathbf u}M_3(p_{\rm core}^{(T-\Gamma_J(\mathbf u))})^{1/4}\right)^4,
\qquad c_{\rm ext}=\frac1{52488}.
\tag{7}
$$
In particular, if \(\mathcal G\subseteq\lbrace0,\ldots,T\rbrace\) and \(\kappa>0\) satisfy \(M_3(p_{\rm core}^{(U)})\ge\kappa8^{-(r+1)}\) for \(U\in\mathcal G\), write \(\alpha_{\mathcal G}=\sum_{\mathbf u:T-\Gamma_J(\mathbf u)\in\mathcal G}\pi_{\mathbf u}\), summing only feasible vectors. Then
$$
M_3(p^{\rm full})\ge c_{\rm ext}\kappa
\left(\frac{\alpha_{\mathcal G}}{\mathcal B_{P,J}}\right)^4 8^{-N}.
\tag{8}
$$
For the all-cutoff specialization, suppose that the same core estimate with coefficient \(\kappa>0\) holds at every feasible residual cutoff, and explicitly choose \(\mathcal G=\lbrace T-\Gamma_J(\mathbf u):\mathbf u\in\lbrace0,1,2\rbrace^J,\ \Gamma_J(\mathbf u)\le T\rbrace\). This choice contains the residual cutoff of every feasible tail vector and therefore gives \(\alpha_{\mathcal G}=1\). It is exactly the cutoff-stable core setting of accepted `6fe9007fe1ba4c4b`, with the same map. Equations (7)-(8) retain cheaper four-site coordinates in that core and concern the full original normalizer \(Z_{\rm full}\).
